WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.435 --> 00:00:03.373
Here is a puzzle you can start solving in ten seconds.

2
00:00:04.264 --> 00:00:05.140
Choose six.

3
00:00:06.029 --> 00:00:08.413
If the number is even, divide it by two.

4
00:00:09.302 --> 00:00:12.099
If it is odd, multiply by three and add one.

5
00:00:12.988 --> 00:00:14.264
Six becomes three.

6
00:00:15.153 --> 00:00:16.222
What comes next?

7
00:00:19.685 --> 00:00:20.162
Ten!

8
00:00:21.052 --> 00:00:25.176
Then five, sixteen, eight, four, two, one.

9
00:00:26.065 --> 00:00:27.818
You have checked one starting number.

10
00:00:28.707 --> 00:00:32.289
The Collatz conjecture says that every positive whole number eventually

11
00:00:32.302 --> 00:00:33.127
reaches one.

12
00:00:34.016 --> 00:00:36.014
Try seven yourself after the video.

13
00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:38.166
The rules stay simple.

14
00:00:39.055 --> 00:00:41.439
The journey can get surprisingly complicated.

15
00:00:43.352 --> 00:00:46.290
Imagine testing an enormous crowd of starting numbers.

16
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.482
Every one comes home.

17
00:00:49.371 --> 00:00:52.335
That still leaves infinitely many untested starts.

18
00:00:53.224 --> 00:00:56.716
A proof must exclude both an endless escape and a different cycle.

19
00:00:57.606 --> 00:01:00.776
One stubborn exception would be enough to defeat the conjecture.

20
00:01:02.685 --> 00:01:04.579
Now step inside our research map.

21
00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:09.271
These twisting ribbons borrow the visual language of protein diagrams.

22
00:01:10.161 --> 00:01:13.911
Their shape helps us explore ideas; it predicts no solution.

23
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.833
Turn the structure, select an argument, and follow its connections.

24
00:01:19.722 --> 00:01:21.733
Color tells you whose work you are reading.

25
00:01:22.622 --> 00:01:24.439
Empty nodes mark open steps.

26
00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:28.439
The green branch follows Terence Tao.

27
00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:33.363
His theorem says that almost all starts reach values below any bound that

28
00:01:33.376 --> 00:01:35.644
grows to infinity, however slowly.

29
00:01:36.534 --> 00:01:40.283
Here, almost all has a precise meaning: logarithmic density.

30
00:01:41.173 --> 00:01:42.590
This is a major result.

31
00:01:43.479 --> 00:01:47.397
But exceptional starts remain possible, and the theorem does not say that

32
00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:48.840
every path reaches one.

33
00:01:50.768 --> 00:01:53.023
The red branch credits other researchers.

34
00:01:53.914 --> 00:01:55.203
Riho Terras and C.

35
00:01:55.216 --> 00:01:55.512
J.

36
00:01:55.525 --> 00:01:59.133
Everett established almost-everywhere descent below the starting number.

37
00:02:00.022 --> 00:02:03.643
Bernstein and Lagarias developed a precise two-adic description of the

38
00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:04.455
dynamics.

39
00:02:05.344 --> 00:02:08.012
These are different tools for different parts of the problem.

40
00:02:08.901 --> 00:02:11.698
This selection is only a small part of the literature.

41
00:02:13.685 --> 00:02:18.724
Blue opens our working archive: exact identities, restricted lemmas, audits,

42
00:02:18.891 --> 00:02:19.974
and failed routes.

43
00:02:20.864 --> 00:02:24.769
Khamit Kadyrbekov and Daniyal Kadirbekov organize this project with AI

44
00:02:24.782 --> 00:02:25.581
assistance.

45
00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:30.052
The new weighted-fiber drafts isolate a residual part that still needs

46
00:02:30.065 --> 00:02:30.710
control.

47
00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:35.465
Blue means a project record, not a claim that every ingredient is new.

48
00:02:36.354 --> 00:02:38.828
Internal checks are not external peer review.

49
00:02:40.768 --> 00:02:42.727
Here is one way a proof could finish.

50
00:02:43.618 --> 00:02:47.252
Show that every odd starting number greater than one eventually falls below

51
00:02:47.265 --> 00:02:47.909
itself.

52
00:02:48.798 --> 00:02:51.582
Strong induction would then carry every start to one.

53
00:02:52.471 --> 00:02:54.662
We do not yet have that universal step.

54
00:02:55.551 --> 00:02:59.262
A large archive does not tell us what percentage is solved, or when the

55
00:02:59.275 --> 00:03:00.757
missing idea will arrive.

56
00:03:02.685 --> 00:03:07.247
This is the Ingenium approach: begin with a small example, make a prediction,

57
00:03:07.414 --> 00:03:10.571
test your reasoning, then find the real research question.

58
00:03:11.462 --> 00:03:13.202
Open the map from the description.

59
00:03:14.091 --> 00:03:14.877
Rotate it.

60
00:03:15.766 --> 00:03:16.746
Enter a branch.

61
00:03:17.635 --> 00:03:19.078
Read an original source.

62
00:03:19.967 --> 00:03:23.975
Then ask: exactly which missing statement would connect this argument to a

63
00:03:23.988 --> 00:03:25.302
proof for every number?
